This project was created with Bridra 0.1 Project Template v2. It keeps Flutter
responsible for the cross-platform interface and Go responsible for middleware,
request validation, controllers, services, models, and transports.

## Development

```bash
fvm install
fvm flutter pub get
make doctor
make verify
make run
```

`make run` builds the Go sidecar, exposes its path to Flutter, and starts the
current Flutter desktop target. Mobile and Web use the HTTP backend:

```bash
make backend-serve
fvm flutter run -d chrome \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/rpc \
  --dart-define=BRIDRA_BACKEND_TOKEN=dev-token
```

The Bridra CLI can supervise the same development session through the project's
versioned framework dependency:

```bash
cd backend
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root ..
go run {{.BridraGoModule}}/cmd/bridra dev --root .. --device chrome
```

Mobile and custom devices require `--listen 0.0.0.0:<port>` plus an explicit
`--backend-url` reachable from the device.

On macOS with full Xcode, exercise the generated iOS app and real Go HTTP
backend in a Simulator or on a trusted physical iPhone:

```bash
make ios-simulator-smoke
make ios-device-smoke
```

The Simulator target verifies Health, Greeting, and ordered Streaming/Progress
through the running iOS app and is suitable for hosted macOS CI.

The device target selects the only available iPhone unless `DEVICE=<id>` is
provided, avoids occupied local ports, verifies Health and Greeting, forces a
backend outage and verifies reconnect, checks ordered Streaming and Progress
before and after recovery, and proves that a signed Profile app cold-launches
twice without Flutter tooling. Configure an Apple Development Team before
running it and accept the one-time Local Network prompt for a new bundle
identifier; signing remains application-owned.

## Builds

Build desktop Sidecar bundles or mobile/Web HTTP artifacts through the same local
Bridra CLI dependency:

```bash
make macos-build
make linux-build
make windows-build

make web-build \
  BACKEND_URL=https://api.example.com/rpc \
  BACKEND_TOKEN=replace-me
```

Set `BUILD_MODE=debug`, `profile`, or `release`. Profile and release HTTP builds
require an HTTPS `/rpc` endpoint and token. Each successful build writes a
token-free SHA-256 manifest under `build/bridra/`. iOS output is unsigned;
signing, notarization, installers, and store submission are separate release
steps.

## Contract generation

`schema/bridra.json` is the RPC source of truth:

```bash
make generate
make codegen-check
```

Set `"stream": true` on a schema method to generate a typed
`Stream<RpcStreamEvent<ResultType>>`; use `framework.ProduceStream` in its Go
Controller.

Use a `"type": "file"` field for large input or output. Stage output through
`framework.FileTransferStoreKey`, then consume its generated `RpcFileReference`
with `BackendGateway.download`. Create input with `RpcFileUpload`, call
`BackendGateway.upload`, and consume it in Go with
`FileTransferStore.ConsumeUpload`. Desktop uses managed temporary files; HTTP
uses short-lived capabilities with byte-range retry. Both paths verify size and
SHA-256.

